// A LITERAL UNION beside an EXTRA LITERAL of the same base — `Kind | "err"` where
// `Kind = "a" | "b"` — spelled INLINE in a function's return type.
//
//   emitProgram: literal `is` over a struct union is not supported
//
// THE DECLARED SPELLING OF THE SAME UNION RAN while this one refused, and the mechanism was
// NOT the one first read off the code. The filed prediction said `litUnionFlatMembers`
// declines a bare `TyLit` member so the annotation route keeps `[Kind, "err"]` — true, but
// the DECLARATION route calls the same helper and it declined there too, so it never
// explained the disagreement. Probed at `emitIs` on both spellings, every reading was
// identical (`exprIsLitAtom=N`, `exprUnion=Y`, `nodeTyIsLitUnion=N`, `tyIsLitUnion=N`,
// `tyLitUnionAliasIx=-1`) except ONE: `unionNameOfExpr` answered `R` for the declared
// spelling and `""` for the inline one. The declared alias has a NAME whose member set the
// emitter can resolve; the inline union has none, and `emitUnionLitIs`' FIRST gate disjunct
// is `uname == ""`. The declared spelling was running on the name channel over an arena
// shape neither route could rep.
//
// CLOSED by teaching `litUnionFlatMembers` that a bare literal of the common base is a
// MEMBER of that set: both routes now mint `"a" | "b" | "err"` and neither needs a name.
//
// MEASURED PRE-EXISTING, not introduced by the D1024 subsumption collapse: a `git archive
// origin/master` build graded both spellings identically (inline refuses, declared runs).
// The collapse does not fire on this union at all, and must not — `Kind | "err"` has no
// `string` ARM (flattening `Kind` reaches its LITERALS), so it is a genuine three-atom
// litunion under the 2026-09-02 ruling. The flatten ADDS the literal to a literal set; the
// collapse DROPS an arm that adds no values. They are different rules on different inputs.
//
// Should print a then b then e.
type Kind = "a" | "b"

function pick(n: i32): Kind | "err" {
  if n == 0 { return "a" }
  if n == 1 { return "b" }
  "err"
}

let i = 0
while i < 3 {
  const r = pick(i)
  if r is "a" {
    print("a")
  } else {
    if r is "b" { print("b") } else { print("e") }
  }
  i = i + 1
}
